FP-Letter-E
Dear San Bernardino County Voter:
Welcome to your San Bernardino County Voter Information Guide for the November 3, 2026, General
Election. As your Registrar of Voters, my priority is to make sure you have clear, accurate information and
convenient options for casting your ballot in a secure and accessible election.
Every active registered voter in San Bernardino County will receive a Vote-by-Mail ballot. The United States
Postal Service will begin delivering ballots on October 5, 2026, giving you time to review your choices and
decide how and when you would like to vote.
You have four convenient options for casting your ballot:
1. Vote by Mail
2. Use a Ballot Drop Box
3. Visit an Early Vote Site
4. Vote in person on Election Day
If you vote by mail, your return postage is prepaid. Your ballot must be postmarked on or before Election Day
to be counted. If mailing close to Election Day, go inside a U.S. Post Office and request a manual postmark.
Please mail your ballot early. Voting early—by mail or in person—allows your ballot to be processed in time
to appear in the first election night results. You may also return your ballot at an Early Vote site or an official
ballot drop box.
Polling places will be open on Election Day, Tuesday, November 3, 2026, from 7 a.m. to 8 p.m. Your
assigned polling place is listed on the back cover of this Guide. However, you may vote in person at any
polling place in San Bernardino County.
If you have questions, call (909) 387-8300, email Communications@rov.sbcounty.gov, or visit our website
Elections.SBCounty.gov, where you can find information about the election including resources, a list of
polling places, Early Vote sites, and ballot drop boxes.
Thank you for participating in the November 3, 2026, General Election. Your vote matters, and we are here to
ensure it is counted—accurately, securely, and on time.
Sincerely,
Joani Finwall
Registrar of Voters
