SB COMPREHENSIVE-002
FP-Intro Ballot-E
What's On Your Ballot
In the 2026 General Election, all voters will receive an Official Ballot. Depending on which voting precinct you
reside in, the following contests and measures may appear on your ballot.
Voter-Nominated Offices
• Includes contests for:
o Statewide Offices
o United States Representative
o Member, State Board of Equalization
o State Senator
o Member of the State Assembly
• The party preference of the candidate is listed on the ballot.
Nonpartisan Offices
• Includes contests for:
o Judicial Offices
o School Offices
o County Superintendent of Schools
o City Offices
o Special District Offices
• The party preference of the candidate is not listed on the ballot.
• Voters may write in the name of a candidate whose name is not on the ballot.
Measures Submitted to the Voters
• State Propositions
• School Bond Measures
• County Measure
• City Measures
• Special District Measures
Note for Measures
• Each measure will include an Impartial Analysis and may include Arguments, Rebuttal Arguments, Tax
Rate Statements and Text of the Measures.
• Not all voting precincts will have local measures.
Information about state propositions is available in your California Official Voter Information Guide and on the
California Secretary of State's website. Visit VoterGuide.sos.ca.gov for more details.
SB COMPREHENSIVE-003
FP-Four Ways-E
Voting Information
Four Ways to Receive Your Ballot Four Ways to Cast Your Voted Ballot
1) A ballot will be mailed to you
• Mail ballots will be delivered to the U.S.
Postal Service on Monday, October 5.
• Request a replacement mail ballot if you do
not receive your ballot, your ballot is
damaged, or you mismark your ballot.
o Call (909) 387-8300 or visit the Registrar of
Voters' website at
Elections.SBCounty.gov.
2) Access a Remote Accessible Vote By Mail
Ballot (RAVBM)
• Voters may use their computer to access and
vote using an accessible ballot. Once
selections are marked, voters will print their
ballot, place it in an envelope, and return it to
the Registrar of Voters office, a Polling Place,
a Ballot Drop Box or Early Vote site.
• To access the RAVBM and the Accessible
Voter Information Guide, visit the Registrar of
Voters' website at Elections.SBCounty.gov.
3) Visit An Early Vote Site
• Visit the Registrar of Voters office from
Monday to Friday, October 5 through
November 2 (8 a.m. - 5 p.m.). The office will
also be open on Saturday, October 31 (8
a.m. - 5 p.m.) and Election Day, November 3
(7 a.m. - 8 p.m.).
• Additional Early Vote sites are open from
Thursday to Monday, October 29 through
November 2 (10 a.m. - 6 p.m.).
• At an Early Vote site, you may vote in
person, register to vote to receive a ballot
and receive accessibility and language
assistance.
4) Visit Your Polling Place
• Polling Places are open on Election Day,
November 3 (7 a.m. - 8 p.m.).
• At a Polling Place, you may vote in person,
register to vote to receive a ballot and receive
accessibility and language assistance.
1) By Mail
• Place your voted ballot in the postage-paid
Official Return Envelope provided.
• Sign and seal the envelope.
• Mail your voted ballot via U.S. Postal Service.
IMPORTANT REMINDER: In order for your
ballot to be eligible to be counted, the return
envelope MUST be postmarked on or before
November 3 and received by the Registrar of
Voters office no later than November 10.
2) Ballot Drop Box
• Place your voted ballot in the postage-paid
Official Return Envelope provided.
• Sign and seal the envelope.
• Drop off your voted ballot at any secure Ballot
Drop Box listed at
Elections.SBCounty.gov/Voting/
MailBallotDropOff.
3) Early Vote Site
• Drop off your voted ballot at any Early Vote
site (see next page for hours and locations).
• At any Early Vote site, you may also register
to vote to receive a ballot and receive
accessibility and language assistance.
4) Polling Place
• Drop off your voted ballot at any Polling Place.
• You may also cast an in-person ballot at any
Polling Place.
• You may also register to vote, to receive a
ballot and receive accessibility and language
assistance.
• Polling Places are open on Election Day,
November 3 (7 a.m. - 8 p.m.).
You may track your mail ballot using California
Secretary of State's tracking tool at:
WheresMyBallot.sos.ca.gov.
In some locations, mail may not receive a
postmark until the following day. If you
mail your ballot on Election Day, it is
recommended that you request a manual
postmark from a postal employee inside
your local U.S. Post Office.
SB COMPREHENSIVE-004
FP-In-Person Locations-E
In-Person Voting Locations
1) San Bernardino County Registrar of Voters
• 777 E. Rialto Avenue, San Bernardino, CA 92415.
• Open Monday through Friday, October 5 to November 2, from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.
• Closed on Monday, October 12 in observance of the holiday.
• Open Saturday, October 31, from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.
• Open Election Day, Tuesday, November 3, from 7 a.m. to 8 p.m.
2) Early Vote Sites
• Vote at any Early Vote site.
• Open Thursday, October 29, to Saturday, October 31, and Monday, November 2, 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.
3) Polling Places
• Open Election Day, Tuesday, November 3, from 7 a.m. to 8 p.m.
• To find your assigned polling place, refer to the back of this guide. You may also go to any polling
place to vote.
For more information:
• Use the My Elections Gateway online application on the Registrar of Voters'
website at Elections.SBCounty.gov.
• Use the Polling Place Look-Up Tool on the Registrar of Voters' website at
Elections.SBCounty.gov/Voting/PPL.
• Call (800) 881-VOTE or (909) 387-8300.
• Scan the QR code to see more Voting Options.
