IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF BOND MEASURE A
This Impartial Analysis of Bond Measure A has been prepared by County Counsel pursuant to Section 9500
of the California Elections Code.
This Measure was placed on the ballot by a resolution adopted by the Board of Education of the Upland Unified School
District (District). A "yes" vote by at least fifty-five percent of the voters voting on this Measure will authorize the District to
issue bonds in the amount of $236,700,000 to be used solely for the purposes set forth in this ballot Measure. A "no" vote
means that the District will not be authorized to issue and sell the $236,700,000 in bonds set forth in the Measure.
The District's Resolution was adopted pursuant to California Education Code sections 15100 et seq., 15264, et seq.,
Government Code section 53506 and Articles XIIIA and XVI of the California Constitution.
The bond proceeds may only be used for the types of projects listed in the Measure which is contained in Exhibit B to the
Resolution. These projects include: constructing, expanding, renovating, modernizing, replacing, upgrading, or removing
classrooms and learning spaces to support current educational programs and improve safety, accessibility and
operational efficiency; various building property maintenance projects and updating/adding restrooms; providing upgraded
or new classrooms, labs, shops, and specialized instructional spaces for job training and career readiness after high
school; constructing or renovating science, technology, engineering and math (STEM) classrooms, labs, and teaching
spaces, student support facilities, facilities to provide administrative services and multi-purpose
rooms/auditoriums/theaters; improving school safety and security through upgraded emergency communications,
cameras, alarms, fencing, emergency response systems, security infrastructure, secure campus entry points, student
safety and traffic flow at parking and school drop-off and pick-up areas; improving facilities for accessibility, including
those required by the Americans with Disabilities Act; and other projects for all grade levels of schools and other District
sites. Some of the projects listed in the Measure may assume the receipt of matching State funds which may or may not
be approved by the State Legislature.
The District will conduct financial and performance audits annually and all bond expenditures will be monitored by an
Independent Citizens' Oversight Committee to ensure that the bond proceeds are spent as promised and specified as
required by Education Code section 15278 et seq. in order to ensure bond revenues are used for proper purposes. No
District employees or vendors will be allowed to serve on the Citizens' Oversight Committee. Bond proceeds cannot be
used for teacher or administrator salaries not related to oversight of the projects.
The District will also take all actions necessary to establish an account in which proceeds from the bond Measure will be
deposited in accordance with state law.
The bonded debt will be a general obligation of the District and will be financed by taxes levied annually on taxable
property located within the District.
