IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF MEASURE E
This Impartial Analysis of Measure E has been prepared by the City Attorney pursuant to Section 9280 of
the California Elections Code.
On June 9, 2026, the Highland City Council unanimously voted to place Measure E on the November ballot. If approved
by the voters, Measure E would authorize a one percent (1%) retail transactions and use tax ("Sales Tax Ordinance")
within the City of Highland. This would increase the City's existing sales tax rate by 1%, or 1 cent, on every dollar of
taxable sales of goods in the City, and on the taxable storage, use or consumption of goods purchased in the City. The
Sales Tax Ordinance would remain effective until ended by the voters.
This sales tax would be a "general tax," meaning that revenues raised from the tax would go into the City's general fund to
pay for any lawful City program, improvement, or service. City staff have estimated that the Sales Tax Ordinance will raise
approximately $6.35 million annually in local revenue. The City Council's adopted ballot question states the revenue from
Measure E would be used to fund police, fire, and emergency services, and repair critical infrastructure such as roads,
bridges, and interchanges, including the 5th Street Corridor Interchange, among other public improvements.
Similar to other sales tax revenue, the State of California will collect revenue generated by the Measure under a contract
with the City, and will pay the revenue over to the City. The City's budget process and annual independent audit, as well
as the City's Annual Comprehensive Financial Report and other publicly available documents, will provide accountability
over revenue and expenditures from the Sales Tax Ordinance. The total sales tax rate paid in the City of Highland, which
includes the sales tax collected for the State of California, San Bernardino County, and other public agencies, is currently
7.75%. Sales tax is not collected on certain exempt items such as prescription medications or groceries.
Article XIIIC of the California Constitution, commonly known as Proposition 218, requires that a majority of the voters
voting on a general tax measure adopt the Sales Tax Ordinance for it to take effect. A "yes" vote on Measure E is to adopt
the Sales Tax Ordinance. A "no" vote on Measure E is to not adopt the Sales Tax Ordinance.
