IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF MEASURE H
This Impartial Analysis of Measure H has been prepared by the City Attorney pursuant to Section 9280 of
the California Elections Code.
On August 7, 2026, the City Council voted to place Measure "H" on the November 3, 2026 General Municipal Election
ballot.
If approved by a majority of Ontario voters, this Measure would authorize a one-half percent (1/2%) retail transactions and
use (sales) tax within the City of Ontario. A one-half percent (1/2%) rate equates to an extra 50¢ per $100 purchased. It
is estimated that Measure "H" will provide an additional $32,500,000 in annual local funding that could be used for
maintaining general City services such as 911 fire, paramedic and police response, anti-gang programs, cleaning and
maintaining public areas, repairing streets and potholes, addressing homelessness, increasing access to affordable
housing for seniors, families and individuals, and retaining and attracting local businesses. This tax would be a "general
tax," meaning that revenues raised from the tax would go into the City's general fund to pay for any lawful City program,
improvement, or service.
California Proposition 218 and Revenue and Taxation Code section 7285.9 authorize the City to levy a general
transactions and use/sales tax at a rate of one-half percent (1/2%) so long as the tax is approved by a majority of the
voters voting in an election on that issue. If approved, the tax would start being collected on April 1, 2027 and would
remain in effect until repealed by voters at a subsequent election.
The tax would be paid in addition to current sales taxes and would be collected at the same time and in the same manner
as existing sales taxes. All revenues raised by the tax would remain in the City and would not be shared with the State,
County or any other agency.
A "yes" vote on Measure "H" will authorize the 1/2% transactions and use (sales) tax.
A "no" vote on Measure "H" will not authorize the 1/2% transactions and use (sales) tax.
The above statement is an impartial analysis of Measure "H". If you desire a copy of the Measure, please call the
City's elections official at (909) 395-2000 and a copy will be mailed at no cost to you.
