IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF MEASURE J
This Impartial Analysis of Measure J has been prepared by the City Attorney pursuant to Section 9280 of the
California Elections Code.
If approved, Measure J would authorize a one percent (1% - one cent on every dollar) transactions and use (sales) tax
within the City of Chino Hills. It is estimated that Measure J will provide an additional $11,500,000.00 annually to the
City's general fund, which is used mainly to fund police services, open space maintenance (including wildfire prevention
services), park and street maintenance, and community programs and events. This tax would be a "general tax,"
meaning that revenues raised from the tax would be deposited into the City's general fund and could be used for any
lawful City purpose.
The sales tax would generally be applicable to retail sales of tangible items. Like the existing sales tax, it would not apply
to sales of certain food products for human consumption, sales of prescription medicine, tuition, rent, or utilities. Labor
and services are also generally exempt from sales tax.
California Revenue and Taxation Code section 7285.9 authorizes the City to levy a general transactions and use (sales)
tax so long as the tax is approved by a majority of the voters voting in an election on the issue. If approved, the tax would
remain in effect until ended by Chino Hills voters voting at a subsequent election. The City Council may also choose to
suspend all or a portion of the tax at any time in its discretion.
The current sales tax rate in Chino Hills is 7.75%, of which the state receives 6%, the City receives 1%, and the county
receives 0.75%. The proposed 1% sales tax would be in addition to the current sales tax rate. However, all revenues
raised by the proposed tax would be controlled by the City and available for local use. Neither the state nor the county
would share in the tax revenue.
The Chino Hills City Council placed Measure J on the ballot. It takes a simple majority of the votes cast to pass Measure
J.
A "yes" vote is a vote in favor of the 1% transactions and use (sales) tax.
A "no" vote is a vote against the 1% transactions and use (sales) tax.
ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF MEASURE J
This Argument in Favor of Measure J was submitted pursuant to Section 9282 of the California Elections
Code. It is the opinion of the author(s), and has been printed exactly as submitted.
Vote Yes on Measure J
Chino Hills is one of the safest and most desirable communities in the region because generations of residents and City
leaders have invested in public safety, wildfire preparedness, streets, parks, open space, and the neighborhood quality of
life. Measure J protects those priorities and keeps Chino Hills strong.
Measure J:
• Maintains 911 emergency response and neighborhood police patrols
• Supports crime prevention and keeps public areas safe
• Strengthens wildfire prevention and emergency preparedness
• Repairs streets, potholes, and aging infrastructure
• Maintains parks, open space, and recreation programs
It does not apply to groceries, prescription medications, rent or mortgage payments, utility service charges, medical or
dental services, tuition, childcare, labor, or personal services.
An estimated 64% of Chino Hills' sales tax revenue is generated by visitors who shop, dine and seek entertainment here.
Visitors strengthen our economy, but they also use local roads, emergency response and public safety services. Measure
J allows visitors to help pay for the services and infrastructure they use.
